    summary: "This session reviews lessons 1 to 12: the plan of the cross, why Jesus had to die, the Last Supper and Gethsemane, betrayal and denial, the trials, the crucifixion, the seven words, the burial, the empty tomb, the appearances, the evidence and the meaning of the resurrection. It prepares learners for the mid-session test.",
    scripture: ["1 Corinthians 15:3-4", "Romans 3:25-26"], more: ["Isaiah 53:5-6", "John 19:30", "Luke 24:5-6"], memory: "1 Corinthians 15:3-4",
    minutes: 60,
    objectives: ["Recall the main events and meaning of the passion and resurrection from weeks 1 to 12", "Link each event to a passage of Scripture", "Prepare for the mid-session test with confidence and thanksgiving"],
    terms: [["Passion", "The suffering and death of Christ."], ["Resurrection", "Christ's bodily rising from the dead on the third day."], ["Propitiation", "A sacrifice that turns away wrath and makes peace with God."]],
    intro: "We have walked together through the most important week in history. We have seen the plan of God, the sin that required a sacrifice, the supper and the garden, the betrayal and denial, the trials, the cross, the burial, the empty tomb and the appearances of the risen Lord. Today we gather those threads and prepare for the test. As you review, ask not only what happened but what it means for you.",
    context: "This is the midpoint of the programme and the default week of the mid-session test, which covers lessons 1 to 12. Churches may hold the test during class time or on another day; where it is on another day, this session serves as a guided review. 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 sums up the first half: Christ died for our sins in accordance with the Scriptures, was buried and was raised on the third day.",
    observe: "Paul's summary states the gospel in four short phrases: died, buried, raised, appeared. The first half of the course followed those events in the Gospels and explained their meaning in Romans, Hebrews and 1 Corinthians. The second half will explore how the Bible pictures the atonement, what the resurrection means for believers and how to live in its power.",
    points: [
      ["The death of Christ was God's plan to deal with sin", "We reviewed Acts 2, Isaiah 53, Romans 3, Hebrews 9 and the events of Luke 22 to 23. He was betrayed, tried, crucified and buried, bearing the penalty of sin as our substitute. Recall Romans 3:25-26, Galatians 3:13 and John 19:30. The cross shows justice and mercy together."],
      ["The resurrection is a historical fact that changes everything", "The tomb was empty, Jesus appeared to many, and the disciples were transformed from fearful men into bold witnesses. Recall Luke 24, John 20 and 1 Corinthians 15:3-8. It vindicates Christ, confirms the gospel, and guarantees our own resurrection."],
      ["Our response is repentance, faith, worship and hope", "We respond to the cross with sorrow for sin and gratitude for forgiveness, and to the resurrection with joy and hope. We share the news with others. This sets up the second half of the course, where we explore the pictures of the atonement and living in the power of the risen Lord."],
    ],
    discuss: [
      ["Which event of the passion or resurrection has most affected you in this course?", "Let learners share. Use answers to review. Ask them to support answers from the passage."],
      ["How would you explain to a friend why Jesus died and rose again?", "Sin, substitute, forgiveness, victory, hope. Let learners give clear, simple explanations."],
      ["Which Scripture passages have helped you the most?", "Isaiah 53, Romans 3, 1 Corinthians 15, Luke 24. Encourage specific and personal answers."],
      ["What questions do you still have?", "Record them for later sessions and encourage practical questions from learners' own contexts."],
    ],
    apply: "Write a one-page summary of the first twelve lessons with a Scripture reference for each key event. Review it twice before the test and tell someone at home the good news of the cross and resurrection. Thank God for each lesson in prayer.",
    activity: "Play a team review game: the teacher reads a clue (for example 'a kiss in the garden') and teams call out the event and the passage. Give extra points for application. End by reading 1 Corinthians 15:3-4 together.",
    takeHome: "Prepare for the test by reviewing the revision map and memory verses. Write five practice questions of your own, with answers, and test someone.",
    prayer: "Thank God for the cross and the empty tomb. Ask for calm minds for the test. Pray that what we have learned will deepen our love for Christ.",
    teacher: "Explain how the mid-session test works: time, number of questions, and when results are released. Reassure anxious learners. If the test is held on another date, use this session as a review. Close with prayer and encouragement.",
    assess: "This unit is the mid-session test week. The mid-session test covers Lessons 1 to 12 and is built from the approved question bank following the curriculum blueprint.",
    mcq1: ["Which verse says Christ died for our sins, was buried and was raised on the third day?", ["1 Corinthians 15:3-4", "John 3:16", "Romans 12:1", "Psalm 23:1"], 0, "1 Corinthians 15:3-4 states the gospel."],
    mcq2: ["What were Jesus' last words recorded in John 19:30?", ["It is finished", "I thirst", "Follow me", "Forgive them"], 0, "He said, 'It is finished.'"],
